course: dictionary
lesson: 4341
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. To know which meaning is right, read the words around it, which is its context. The word guernsey has four meanings: a place, a jumper, a sports shirt and a cow.
explain: «The Isle of Man, Jersey and Guernsey are Crown Dependencies, not part of the UK.» Here Guernsey is a place, because it sits in a list with other islands and the sentence is about where they belong. Look for clues like this before you decide.
explain: Capital letters help too. Guernsey with a capital G is a place or a breed of cow, while a guernsey with a small g is a jumper or, in Australia, a football jersey. Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence and asking if it still makes sense.
fact: Guernsey is one of the Channel Islands, between England and France.
fact: A guernsey is a thick, warm, tightly knitted woollen jumper, first worn by fishermen.
fact: In Australia, a guernsey is a sleeveless jersey worn by football players.
fact: In Australia, to get a guernsey means to be picked for a team or to get recognition.
fact: A Guernsey is a brown-and-white dairy cow.
exercise: «The Isle of Man, Jersey and Guernsey are Crown Dependencies.» In this sentence, what is Guernsey? | A brown-and-white cow | A sleeveless football jersey | A thick woollen jumper | *A place, one of the Channel Islands
exercise: «Grandma knitted a warm guernsey for the fishermen.» What is a guernsey here? | *A thick, warm woollen jumper | A place in the sea | A pair of football boots | A cow in a field
exercise: «Mia was thrilled to get a guernsey for the footy team.» What does get a guernsey mean here? | To win a race | *To be picked for a team | To knit a jumper | To move to an island
test: «The farmer's brown-and-white Guernsey gave lots of milk.» What is a Guernsey here? | A football shirt | *A breed of dairy cow | A jumper | An island
test: Ella asks, «Is a guernsey the same as a Guernsey?» What is the best answer? | *No: small g is a jumper, capital G is a place or a cow breed | Yes, they always mean the same | Yes, both are Channel Islands | No, a guernsey is a cow
exam: «The guernsey on the peg was thick and warm, and Guernsey lies between England and France.» Which meaning of guernsey is used in the first part? | A place | A cow | *A thick, warm woollen jumper | A football jersey
